ildren":"Use comparison before commitment"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Read tradition-to-tradition comparisons so your path is chosen with clarity rather than mood or aesthetics alone."}]]}],["$","div","Let practice confirm resonance",{"className":"rounded-2xl border border-white/5 bg-black/10 p-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"text-orange-400 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide mb-3","children":["Step ",4]}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold mb-3","children":"Let practice confirm resonance"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"The right path becomes clear through lived experience — what steadies your mind, opens your heart, and deepens sincerity over time."}]]}]]}]]}]}]
17:["$","section",null,{"className":"pt-16 border-t border-border","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"text-center mb-12","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-3xl font-bold mb-4","children":"Frequently Asked Questions"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground max-w-2xl mx-auto","children":"Common questions from seekers exploring Shaiva, Vaishnava, Shakta, and other Dharmic paths."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6","children":[["$","div","What is a Sampradaya?",{"className":"rounded-2xl border border-border/60 bg-card p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-lg font-semibold mb-3","children":"What is a Sampradaya?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"A Sampradaya is a living spiritual lineage — a tradition that carries teachings, rituals, mantras, interpretations, and methods from teacher to student across generations."}]]}],["$","div","Do I need to choose one Hindu tradition forever?",{"className":"rounded-2xl border border-border/60 bg-card p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-lg font-semibold mb-3","children":"Do I need to choose one Hindu tradition forever?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Not at the beginning. Early exploration is natural. Over time, most seekers benefit from depth in one coherent path rather than collecting disconnected practices from many traditions at once."}]]}],["$","div","Can someone honor both Shiva and Vishnu?",{"className":"rounded-2xl border border-border/60 bg-card p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-lg font-semibold mb-3","children":"Can someone honor both Shiva and Vishnu?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Yes. Many practitioners do, and traditions like Smartism explicitly frame multiple deities as valid manifestations of one ultimate reality. The difference is often one of devotional center, not mutual exclusion."}]]}],["$","div","How do I know whether inquiry or devotion suits me?",{"className":"rounded-2xl border border-border/60 bg-card p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-lg font-semibold mb-3","children":"How do I know whether inquiry or devotion suits me?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Ask what feels most alive: investigating the nature of the self, or cultivating loving relationship with the Divine. Most seekers contain both, but one usually acts as the primary doorway."}]]}]]}]]}]
